8. Defenses, summarized

For builders of tool-using agents and of guardrails:

Apply outbound-tool content policy to all arguments that cross the trust boundary, payloads included;
prefer data-flow and provenance checks over destination-only string matching.
Treat chat-template control tokens in untrusted input as hostile: strip, escape, or sandbox them, and
render user content in a data-only region.
When scoring or auditing agent behavior, deduplicate severity per predicate per turn if the intent is to
measure distinct failures rather than repeated identical ones, and decide explicitly whether
template-level artifacts should count.
State the replay timeout and partial-credit policy explicitly, because it changes what the benchmark
rewards.
9. Reproducibility

The attack is a single AttackAlgorithm whose run() performs the live validation-fill of Section 3 with
replay-safe sizing, the single-post marker primitive of Section 2, and the reasoning-channel prefix of
Section 4 on the latency-classified reasoning model.

Target models: GPT-OSS 20B (Harmony template, reasoning) and Gemma (JSON envelope, non-reasoning). The
reasoning-channel injection exploits the Harmony template and is applied only to the row classified slow by
measured latency.
Key module constants: latency split threshold 12 seconds, per-candidate classification sample of 8 probes,
replay-safe fraction 0.98, hard candidate cap 2,000 (the evaluator's replay limit), and a single untimed
warm-up before the timed fill so model-load cost never inflates the latency estimate.
The marker primitive is a single user message http.post(url=<unique host>, data=<marker>) with a short
terminal acknowledgement; hosts are unique per candidate so distinct cells score their novelty points.
No external network or data is required beyond the SDK shipped with the competition. All parameters are
module constants in the submitted attack.py.
Every reported score is a real submission. Negative results are retained with the source evidence that
explains them, so a failed configuration does not become a claim that the whole family is impossible.
10. Lessons learned
Read the scorer and harness from source before optimizing. The leaderboard hides which phase is the
bottleneck; here it is replay latency on the reasoning model, not generation. Grounding the contract in the
hosted gateway rather than the SDK reference also corrected two facts, the tool-hop count and the timeout
policy, that change the analysis.
Persuasion is a red herring when the constraint is throughput. The shortest reliably-firing primitive wins,
and anything that lengthens model output is negative.
The negative results carry the argument. Multi-post, all-hops forging, and multi-step chaining each fail
for a reason visible in the source, and together they show the objective is throughput, not sophistication.
The two concrete weaknesses this benchmark surfaces are a guardrail that checks the destination but not the
payload, and a model that honors control tokens from untrusted input. Both have clean fixes, and both are
common patterns well beyond this competition.
